          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Witten genus
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
            in one variable, playing the role of a universal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+genus"} elliptic genus
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Witten87"} Witten 87
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+volume+limit"} large volume limit
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            (hence in the string worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            about constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            configurations). Specifically, for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring"} type II superstring
            this reproduces the universal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+genus"} elliptic genus
            as previously introduced by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serge+Ochanine"} Serge Ochanine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string"} heterotic string
            it yields what is now called the Witten genus proper. Concretely, as Witten argued, this is a formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
            in string oscillation modes of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-hat+genus"} A-hat genus
            of the symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+powers"} tensor powers
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            that these modes take values in.
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Witten86"}> Witten 86
            ) it is suggested, by regarding the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+loop+space"} smooth loop space
            of its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+volume+limit"} large volume limit
            of an
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-hat+genus"} A-hat genus
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+loop+space"} smooth loop space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac-Ramond+operator"} Dirac-Ramond operator
            in that limit. (Ever since this suggestion people have tried to make precise the concept of Dirac operator on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+loop+space"} smooth loop space
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#AlvarezKillingbackManganoWindey87"} Alvarez-Killingback-Mangano-Windey 87
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+loop+space"} formal loop space
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac-Ramond+operator"} Dirac-Ramond operator
            really appears in the definition of the Witten genus.)
          %p
            A priori the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            of the Witten genus as a genus on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
            manifolds are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi M
                  %msub
                    %mi SO
                    %mo •
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℚ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  w \;\colon\; M SO_\bullet \longrightarrow \mathbb{Q}[ [ q ] ]
                  \\,.
          %p
            In the construction from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string physics
            this map is interpreted as sending a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            to the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                      %mi τ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w_X(q) = w_X(e^{2 \pi i \tau})
            which to each modulus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau \in \mathbb{C}
            characterizing a toroidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            assigns the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo +
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mi τ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}/(\mathbb{Z} + \mathbb{Z}\tau)
            and propagating on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            On manifolds with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            the genus refines to integral power series (via the integrality of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-hat+genus"} A-hat genus
            (
            %a{:href => "#ChudnovskyChudnovsky88"}> Chudnovsky-Chudnovsky 88
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#KreckStolz93"} Kreck-Stolz 93
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Hovey91"} Hovey 91
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+forms"} modular forms
            (
            %a{:href => "#Zagier86"}> Zagier 86
            ) and crucially, on manifolds with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
            it takes values in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+modular+forms"} topological modular forms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi M
                          %msub
                            %mi String
                            %mo •
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %msub
